# Methodology

## 1. Data preparation

1. Loaded all files as text to avoid wrong type guessing.
2. Profiled each table: row counts, null share, duplicate rows, spelling variants.
3. Cleaned IDs, dates, money fields and categories (see the data dictionary).
4. Removed duplicate rows and internal or test accounts from every table.
5. Recovered missing signup dates from signup_completed events.
6. Logged every issue and its fix in a data-quality report.

## 2. Validation

Checks run after cleaning:

- User IDs are unique, and every ID in other tables exists in users.
- End date is not before start date, and no start date is after 2026-09-30.
- MRR is positive, and status and event names are in the approved lists.
- The MRR bridge reconciles: opening + new + expansion - contraction - churned = closing.
- Python, SQL, Power BI and Excel agree on sample months (Excel validation sheet).

## 3. Metric definitions

| Metric | Definition |
|--------|------------|
| Active customer | Has a subscription with start date on or before month end, and end date empty or after month end |
| MRR | Sum of monthly price of active subscriptions at month end (annual price divided by 12) |
| ARR | MRR x 12 |
| ARPA | MRR / active customers |
| New MRR | Customer had no MRR last month and has MRR this month |
| Expansion / Contraction MRR | Existing customer's MRR rose / fell but stayed above zero |
| Churned MRR | Customer had MRR last month and none this month |
| Logo churn rate | Customers lost in the month / customers active at the start of the month |
| Revenue churn rate | Churned MRR / opening MRR |
| NRR | (Opening + expansion - contraction - churned) / opening MRR |
| GRR | (Opening - contraction - churned) / opening MRR |
| Activation | Created a project within 7 days of signup |
| Cohort | Month of the customer's first paid subscription |
| Retention at month k | Share of the cohort still active at the end of month (cohort month + k) |
| CAC | Total channel spend / paying customers from that channel |
| LTV (formula) | ARPA x gross margin / monthly churn rate |
| LTV:CAC | LTV / CAC (3x or more is the usual benchmark) |
| CAC payback | CAC / (ARPA x gross margin), in months |

## 4. Analysis methods

- **Funnel:** strict funnel; a user counts at a step only if every earlier step was completed.
- **Cohorts:** retention matrix and curves by channel, plan and billing cycle.
- **Churn:** by month, tenure bucket, plan, channel and billing cycle. Churn is split into voluntary and
  involuntary; involuntary means the last payment attempt failed and was never recovered.
- **Survival analysis:** Kaplan-Meier curves, which handle customers who have not churned yet.
- **Segmentation:** RFM segments (recency, frequency, monetary) and K-Means clustering on behaviour features.
- **Churn prediction:** monthly snapshots; features use only data up to the snapshot month; the label is
  "not active next month". Time-based train/test split. Compared against a "no logins" baseline and
  evaluated with PR-AUC, not accuracy.
- **A/B testing:** a template with sample size, sample ratio mismatch check, z-test and confidence interval,
  run on simulated data.
- **Forecast:** damped Holt trend model, back-tested on the last 6 months against naive and linear baselines.

## 5. Assumptions

- Gross margin is 80%.
- The data window ends on 2026-09-30.
- Missing marketing spend is interpolated within each channel.
- CAC uses total spend over the whole period divided by total paying customers per channel.
- A customer who returns after a gap is counted as new in that month's bridge.
- A 40% recovery rate is used only for the failed-payment savings scenario.

## 6. Limitations

- The data is synthetic, so behavioural signals are weaker than in real product data.
- Every paying user activated first, so the activation comparison is 100% vs 0% by construction.
- CAC is channel-level and not lagged; real attribution is more complex.
- Observational data shows correlation only; the proposed A/B tests are needed for causal claims.
- The churn model is a benchmark; its precision is modest.
